# X86-SSA-REMAP-POST-FIX — Post-fix audit of per-block remap in oodac/x86_ssa_emit.oo

File: openOODA/audit/x86-ssa-remap-post-fix.oot
Date: 2026-08-25. Status: smoke.

Method: End-to-end read of ooda/oodac/x86_ssa_emit.oo (243 lines, was 230 pre-fix, +13). Cross-check the 2 declared root causes (duplicate labels in println_int, undefined labels in br/cond_br) against the new code. Tabulate every caller of x86_ssa_emit_println_int and confirm argument count. Trace block_names from definition to use site. No file edits, no compiler runs, no /tmp writes.

## 1. Per-bug fix verification

| # | Bug | Expected behavior | Actual behavior | Evidence |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| 1 | Duplicate labels in x86_ssa_emit_println_int: every inline expansion emitted the same `.Lxss_pi_nz`, `.Lxss_pi_print`, `.Lxss_pi_pos`, `.Lxss_pi_loop` label set, so a function with two println_int calls hit `label redefined` at `as` time. | The function takes a per-call-site suffix and embeds it in every internal label. Two call sites in one function yield two disjoint label sets. | Signature is now `fn x86_ssa_emit_println_int(arg_slot: Int, suffix: String) -> String` (line 107). The body at line 109 carries the suffix through 4 label definitions (`.Lxss_pi_nz_<suffix>`, `.Lxss_pi_pos_<suffix>`, `.Lxss_pi_loop_<suffix>`, `.Lxss_pi_print_<suffix>`) and through 2 forward / 1 backward edge. The two callers (see §2) pass distinct suffixes, so labels do not collide. | x86_ssa_emit.oo:107 (signature); x86_ssa_emit.oo:109 (suffixed label sites). |
| 2 | Undefined labels in br / cond_br: branches emitted `jmp .L<fn>_block<imm>` and `jne .L<fn>_block<imm>`, but the actual block labels were emitted as `".L" + name + "_" + bname` using the block name, not `block<imm>`. `as` failed with `undefined symbol`. | Branch instructions target the real per-block label by resolving `imm` (the target block id) through `block_names`. | `x86_ssa_format_inst` declares `block_names: List[String]` (line 78), the caller `x86_ssa_emit_function` forwards `(fn_data).block_names` as `names` (line 30) and then as `names` again at line 52, and the format helper computes `tbname = list_get(block_names, imm)` (line 83). Branches at lines 96 and 97 emit `jmp .L<fn_name>_<tbname>` and `jne .L<fn_name>_<tbname>`. The defining site is line 44 (`".L" + (fn_data).name + "_" + bname`). The two halves match. | x86_ssa_emit.oo:30 (forward block_names), x86_ssa_emit.oo:44 (definition), x86_ssa_emit.oo:52 (forward into format), x86_ssa_emit.oo:78 (param), x86_ssa_emit.oo:83 (lookup), x86_ssa_emit.oo:96-97 (use). |

Both root causes are fixed. No new regressions observed in the 13 added lines.

## 2. New signature consistency check

| Caller | File:line | Args passed | Expected | Actual |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `x86_ssa_format_inst` (opcode 23 println_int branch) | x86_ssa_emit.oo:101 | `(imm, inst_id.to_string())` | 2 args: Int slot + String suffix | 2 args. `inst_id` is the per-instruction index, unique per call site within one function. |
| `x86_ssa_emit_match_program` (terminal match-result println) | x86_ssa_emit.oo:240 | `(3, "match")` | 2 args: Int slot + String suffix | 2 args. Slot 3 maps to `-24(%rbp)`, which the match epilogue writes at line 239 (`movq %rax, -24(%rbp)`). The constant suffix `"match"` is safe because each match program emits one self-contained snippet. |

Both call sites pass exactly 2 args. The new signature is consistent.

## 3. Issues found

1. Unconditional `tbname` lookup (minor). `x86_ssa_format_inst` evaluates `let tbname: String = list_get(block_names, imm);` at line 83 for every instruction, but only opcodes 17 (`br`) and 18 (`cond_br`) read it. The result is dead for the other 22 opcodes. Cost: one extra `list_get` per instruction. Acceptable, flagging for completeness.

2. Visibility of `x86_ssa_emit_println_int` (note). The helper is declared `fn` (private, line 107) yet is called from a `pub fn` (`x86_ssa_emit_match_program`, line 240). Same-file visibility makes this legal under the current module layout. If a future split moves the match program into a sibling module, the private helper will need promotion to `pub fn` or to be co-located with the caller.

3. Suffix collision across repeated `x86_ssa_emit_match_program` calls (defended). The match path always uses the literal suffix `"match"`. If a single function ever held two distinct match lowers, both would emit the same `.Lxss_pi_*_match` label set and re-trigger bug 1. Today the IR has at most one match block per function, so the constant suffix is safe. This assumption deserves a one-line comment at the call site to prevent future drift.

## 4. Recommended follow-ups

- Add a one-line Academy note above `x86_ssa_emit_println_int` (around line 107) stating the suffix must be unique per inline expansion within a single function. No behavior change.
- Hoist the `tbname` lookup into the `op == 17 || op == 18` branches so non-branch instructions stop touching `block_names`. Pure cleanup, no semantic change.
- Promote `x86_ssa_emit_println_int` to `pub fn`, or document its module-private status in the Setup beat, so future module splits do not silently break the match path.
- Add a smoke script under `ooda/scripts/` that compiles a function with two `println_int` calls plus a multi-block `br`, then runs `as` and `ld` to prove no `label redefined` or `undefined symbol` errors. Out of scope for this audit.
- Mirror the `tbname` indirection for any future per-block label scheme (e.g. loop headers) so block id and block name stay linked through one lookup, not two parallel arrays.
